-- chem/fine.cub — hydrogen's fine structure from Dirac's equation, exactly, two ways that agree (docs/chemical-identity.md,
-- the keystone, (k2): below the rest energy). Below, x = (Zα)², and every series is in x with exact binary rational
-- coefficients, truncated at order N: sum, product, inverse of a series with nonzero constant term, and √(1 + u) for u
-- with zero constant term by the binomial series Σ_k C(½, k) u^k, C(½, 0) = 1, C(½, k) = C(½, k−1)(3 − 2k)/(2k).
-- (1) Dirac's level of a hydrogen-like ion (Darwin, Proc. R. Soc. A 118 (1928) 654; Gordon, Z. Phys. 48 (1928) 11):
--   E = mc² [1 + x/(n − δ)²]^{−1/2},   δ = κ − √(κ² − x) = κ(1 − √(1 − x/κ²)),   κ = j + ½,
-- expanded in x: E/mc² = 1 + f₁x + f₂x² + …, where mc²·x = Z² hartree and mc²·x² = Z⁴α² hartree (mc²α² is the hartree).
-- The expansion gives f₁ = −1/(2n²), Schrödinger's level −Z²/(2n²) hartree, and f₂ = −(1/(2n⁴))(n/κ − 3/4).
-- (2) The order-x² term from Foldy and Wouthuysen's reduction (Phys. Rev. 78 (1950) 29): in atomic units the
-- operator is p²/2 + V + α²(−p⁴/8 + ∇²V/8 + σ·(∇V × p)/4), V = −Z/r, so at first order in α² the level shifts by
-- α² times the expectation in the hydrogenic state (n, l, j) of
--   −p⁴/8 = −½(E − V)², with E = −Z²/(2n²), ⟨1/r⟩ = Z/n², ⟨1/r²⟩ = 2Z²/(n³(2l+1)),
--   ∇²V/8 = (π/2)Z δ³(r), with |ψ(0)|² = Z³/(πn³) at l = 0 and 0 otherwise, so Z⁴/(2n³) at l = 0,
--   σ·(∇V × p)/4 = (Z/(2r³)) L·S, with ⟨1/r³⟩ = 2Z³/(n³ l(l+1)(2l+1)) for l ≥ 1 and L·S = l/2 at j = l + ½,
--   −(l+1)/2 at j = l − ½,
-- the hydrogenic expectations as in Bethe and Salpeter, Quantum Mechanics of One- and Two-Electron Atoms (1957),
-- §3. The sum is a coefficient of Z⁴α² hartree; it is checked equal to Dirac's f₂ at every n ≤ 6, l < n and
-- j = l ± ½ (chem/fineclaim.cub). Everything is with Z = 1, the coefficients being Z-free.
import "he.cub"

